# https://spdx.org/rdf/3/terms/Core/RelationshipType/follows node1329
node1329 [ shape=none, color=black label=< <table color='#666666' cellborder='0' cellspacing='0' border='1'><tr><td colspan='2' bgcolor='grey'><B>follows</B></td></tr><tr><td href='https://spdx.org/rdf/3/terms/Core/RelationshipType/follows' bgcolor='#eeeeee' colspan='2'><font point-size='10' color='#6666ff'>https://spdx.org/rdf/3/terms/Core/RelationshipType/follows</font></td></tr><tr><td align='left'>rdfs:comment</td><td align='left'>&quot;The to Element succeeds the from Element, establishing a unidirectional sequence. This succession is defined as chronological, procedural, or logical. It is used to represent either a temporal order (e.g., in a workflow) or a logical order for processing and traversal (e.g., in an ordered list).&quot;@en</td></tr><tr><td align='left'>skos:definition</td><td align='left'>&quot;The to Element succeeds the from Element, establishing a unidirectional sequence. This succession is defined as chronological, procedural, or logical. It is used to represent either a temporal order (e.g., in a workflow) or a logical order for processing and traversal (e.g., in an ordered list).&quot;@en</td></tr></table> > ] 
# https://spdx.org/rdf/3/terms/Software/ContentIdentifierType/gitoid node1330
node1330 [ shape=none, color=black label=< <table color='#666666' cellborder='0' cellspacing='0' border='1'><tr><td colspan='2' bgcolor='grey'><B>gitoid</B></td></tr><tr><td href='https://spdx.org/rdf/3/terms/Software/ContentIdentifierType/gitoid' bgcolor='#eeeeee' colspan='2'><font point-size='10' color='#6666ff'>https://spdx.org/rdf/3/terms/Software/ContentIdentifierType/gitoid</font></td></tr><tr><td align='left'>rdfs:comment</td><td align='left'>&quot;Gitoid &lt;https://www.iana.org/assignments/uri-schemes/prov/gitoid&gt;, stands for Git Object ID &lt;https://git-scm.com/book/en/v2/Git-Internals-Git-Objects&gt;. A gitoid of type blob is a unique hash of a binary artifact. A gitoid may represent either an Artifact Identifier &lt;https://github.com/omnibor/spec/blob/eb1ee5c961c16215eb8709b2975d193a2007a35d/spec/SPEC.md#artifact-identifier-types&gt; for the software artifact or an Input Manifest Identifier &lt;https://github.com/omnibor/spec/blob/eb1ee5c961c16215eb8709b2975d193a2007a35d/spec/SPEC.md#input-manifest-identifier&gt; for the software artifact&#x27;s associated Artifact Input Manifest &lt;https://github.com/omnibor/spec/blob/eb1ee5c961c16215eb8709b2975d193a2007a35d/spec/SPEC.md#artifact-input-manifest&gt;; this ambiguity exists because the Artifact Input Manifest is itself an artifact, and the gitoid of that artifact is its valid identifier. Gitoids calculated on software artifacts (Snippet, File, or Package Elements) should be recorded in the SPDX 3 SoftwareArtifact&#x27;s contentIdentifier property. Gitoids calculated on the Artifact Input Manifest (Input Manifest Identifier) should be recorded in the SPDX 3 Element&#x27;s externalIdentifier property. See OmniBOR Specification &lt;https://github.com/omnibor/spec/&gt;, a minimalistic specification for describing software Artifact Dependency Graphs &lt;https://github.com/omnibor/spec/blob/eb1ee5c961c16215eb8709b2975d193a2007a35d/spec/SPEC.md#artifact-dependency-graph-adg&gt;.&quot;@en</td></tr><tr><td align='left'>skos:definition</td><td align='left'>&quot;Gitoid &lt;https://www.iana.org/assignments/uri-schemes/prov/gitoid&gt;, stands for Git Object ID &lt;https://git-scm.com/book/en/v2/Git-Internals-Git-Objects&gt;. A gitoid of type blob is a unique hash of a binary artifact. A gitoid may represent either an Artifact Identifier &lt;https://github.com/omnibor/spec/blob/eb1ee5c961c16215eb8709b2975d193a2007a35d/spec/SPEC.md#artifact-identifier-types&gt; for the software artifact or an Input Manifest Identifier &lt;https://github.com/omnibor/spec/blob/eb1ee5c961c16215eb8709b2975d193a2007a35d/spec/SPEC.md#input-manifest-identifier&gt; for the software artifact&#x27;s associated Artifact Input Manifest &lt;https://github.com/omnibor/spec/blob/eb1ee5c961c16215eb8709b2975d193a2007a35d/spec/SPEC.md#artifact-input-manifest&gt;; this ambiguity exists because the Artifact Input Manifest is itself an artifact, and the gitoid of that artifact is its valid identifier. Gitoids calculated on software artifacts (Snippet, File, or Package Elements) should be recorded in the SPDX 3 SoftwareArtifact&#x27;s contentIdentifier property. Gitoids calculated on the Artifact Input Manifest (Input Manifest Identifier) should be recorded in the SPDX 3 Element&#x27;s externalIdentifier property. See OmniBOR Specification &lt;https://github.com/omnibor/spec/&gt;, a minimalistic specification for describing software Artifact Dependency Graphs &lt;https://github.com/omnibor/spec/blob/eb1ee5c961c16215eb8709b2975d193a2007a35d/spec/SPEC.md#artifact-dependency-graph-adg&gt;.&quot;@en</td></tr></table> > ] 
# https://spdx.org/rdf/3/terms/Security/VexVulnAssessmentRelationship node1331
node1331 [ shape=none, color=black label=< <table color='#666666' cellborder='0' cellspacing='0' border='1'><tr><td colspan='2' bgcolor='grey'><B>VexVulnAssessmentRelationship</B></td></tr><tr><td href='https://spdx.org/rdf/3/terms/Security/VexVulnAssessmentRelationship' bgcolor='#eeeeee' colspan='2'><font point-size='10' color='#6666ff'>https://spdx.org/rdf/3/terms/Security/VexVulnAssessmentRelationship</font></td></tr><tr><td align='left'>rdfs:comment</td><td align='left'>&quot;Abstract ancestor class for all VEX relationships.&quot;@en</td></tr><tr><td align='left'>skos:definition</td><td align='left'>&quot;Abstract ancestor class for all VEX relationships.&quot;@en</td></tr><tr><td align='left'>skos:note</td><td align='left'>&quot;VexVulnAssessmentRelationship is an abstract subclass that defined the common
properties shared by all the SPDX-VEX status relationships.

*Constraints*

When linking elements using a VexVulnAssessmentRelationship, the following
requirements shall be observed:

- The to: end shall point to elements representing the VEX *products*.

To specify a different element where the vulnerability was detected, the VEX
relationship can optionally specify *subcomponents* using the assessedElement
property.

VEX inherits information from the document level down to its statements. When a
statement is missing information it can be completed by reading the equivalent
field from the containing document. For example, if a VEX relationship is
missing data in its createdBy property, tools shall consider the entity
listed in the CreationInfo section of the document as the VEX author.
In the same way, when a VEX relationship does not have a created property,
the document&#x27;s date shall be considered as authoritative.&quot;@en</td></tr></table> > ] 
